"A CQRS diagram separates command handling from query handling and makes synchronization between their models explicit.\n\nUse when: Review read and write responsibilities with different access needs. Explain eventual consistency between authoritative writes and projections.\n\nAvoid when: A simple shared model already serves the workload clearly.\n\nVisual grammar: Separate paths encode commands that change state and queries that retrieve views. Write and read models are named even when they share physical storage. Projection arrows state how read views receive committed changes.\n\nBuild: Identify authoritative command behavior and query consumers. Draw command validation and write persistence separately from read access. Add projection update and rebuild paths where applicable. Use parallel lanes with synchronization connectors routed between padded model boundaries.\n\nCheck: Queries do not silently mutate the write model. A separate read database is not assumed to be mandatory. Projection lag and read-after-write behavior are stated. Command, query, and projection labels never overlap.\n\nPitfalls: Equating CQRS with event sourcing in every implementation. Drawing separate stores without explaining consistency or rebuild behavior.\n\nExample: PlaceOrder updates the write model; a projection updates OrderSummary, which serves customer order-history queries.\n\nAlternatives: Event sourcing diagram, Data lineage diagram."
